---
/**
 * /systems/hospital-operations — the flagship case study.
 *
 * Structure, section order, and the constitutional sections (header,
 * decisions, limitations, outcomes) all come from CaseStudyLayout. This file
 * supplies only the prose that is specific to this system. Phase 5 writes two
 * more files shaped exactly like this one.
 */
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import HospitalArchitecture from '../../components/diagrams/HospitalArchitecture.astro';

const entry = await getEntry('systems', 'hospital-operations');
if (!entry) throw new Error('Missing content entry: systems/hospital-operations');

/**
 * Gallery assets this case study still needs (blueprint §4.1 asset list), with
 * the sanitisation each one requires (§8.3).
 *
 * These become HTML comments in the built page, where `scripts/copy-check.mjs`
 * reports them on every run and Phase 8 treats any survivor as a launch
 * blocker. They are injected with `set:html` rather than written as literal
 * comments in the template because a Fragment whose only children are comments
 * renders as empty — Astro treats it as having no content, so the markers
 * would silently never reach the output.
 *
 * The page is complete without these images by design. When they arrive, add a
 * `gallery` slot of <Figure demoTenant> elements and the gallery section
 * renders itself. Do not ship empty frames waiting to be filled.
 */
const SANITISATION =
  'Demo or staging tenant only. No real staff, patient, or guest names; no real hospital ' +
  'name or logo in frame; no photographs of actual people; browser chrome cropped or a clean ' +
  'profile with no bookmarks, extensions, or console visible.';

const PENDING_GALLERY = [
  'Screenshot 1 of 5 — the QR scan and cleaning submission flow, mobile 390x844, showing the location resolved from the scan and the photo-capture step.',
  'Screenshot 2 of 5 — the supervisor dashboard, desktop 1440x900, showing the day rounds and their status.',
  'Screenshot 3 of 5 — a maintenance ticket through its lifecycle, desktop 1440x900, ideally a publicly reported fault at the point of resolution. No guest contact details in frame.',
  'Screenshot 4 of 5 — a NABH compliance report exported to PDF and captured as an image. Redact every figure identifying the institution. If redaction leaves it unreadable, omit it rather than ship a censored-looking page.',
  'Screenshot 5 of 5 — the self-service attendance kiosk, mobile 390x844, at the point of capture. Demo worker record; no real selfie of a real person.',
];

const ownerInputMarkers = PENDING_GALLERY.map(
  (asset) => `<!-- OWNER-INPUT: ${asset} ${SANITISATION} -->`,
).join('\n');
---

<CaseStudyLayout entry={entry}>
  <Fragment slot="context">
    <p>
      Housekeeping in a hospital is not judged on whether the work happened. It is judged on whether
      anyone can show that it happened — to a supervisor at the end of a shift, and to an
      accreditation assessor months later. That evidence problem is what this platform exists to
      solve.
    </p>
    <p>
      It runs the housekeeping operation at a NABH-accredited hospital: cleaning rounds anchored to
      a physical location by QR code and submitted with photographs, maintenance faults that anyone
      standing in front of one can report, staff attendance and leave, and the accreditation
      reporting that pulls all of it together.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three groups use it and only one of them has an account. Admins configure locations, staff,
      shifts, and reports. Supervisors log in from the floor to submit cleaning records and resolve
      tickets. Patients, nurses, and housekeeping workers use it without ever registering — which is
      the constraint that shaped most of what follows.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="constraints">
    <p>
      <strong>No team.</strong> Every decision here was made, implemented, deployed, and then carried
      by one person. That changes what is worth building: anything demanding constant attention is a bad
      trade even when it is the better engineering.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>A budget of one machine.</strong> The API, the database, and the reverse proxy are three
      containers on a single virtual machine.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Users who will not sign up.</strong> A patient reporting a broken light will not create
      an account, and neither will a worker clocking in at the start of a shift. The design had to work
      for people who never authenticate at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Evidence people have a reason to fake.</strong> Cleaning records and attendance both attest
      to work being done, by people whose shifts depend on that work being recorded. A system holding
      that kind of claim has to assume it will be lied to.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Two compliance regimes at once.</strong> India's DPDP Act governs the personal data flowing
      through it; NABH accreditation governs what the hospital has to be able to demonstrate about its
      own cleaning.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="architecture">
    <HospitalArchitecture />
    <div class="prose body-copy lifecycle">
      <p>
        A request reaches Caddy, which terminates TLS and forwards to Fastify. Fastify binds to
        localhost and nothing else, so the proxy is the only thing that can reach it even if a cloud
        firewall rule were wrong one day.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then the part that matters. The token is verified, and the user is reloaded from the
        database rather than trusted from the token's own claims — so a disabled account stops
        working immediately instead of at expiry. The organisation is checked as active. Only then
        does the handler run, and every query it makes is scoped by the organisation id taken from
        that verified token. A client can supply an organisation id; the server will compare it and
        reject a mismatch, but it will never use it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Photographs go to object storage, but they are never served from it directly — the bucket
        stays private and images are proxied back through the application, so access to a photograph
        is still a decision the application makes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="security">
    <p>
      One organisation is one hospital, and it is the root of everything. Nearly every row carries
      its id, and where a natural key could collide between two organisations — a staff email, an
      employee number, a shift name — uniqueness is scoped to the organisation rather than declared
      globally. The isolation is in the schema, not only in the queries.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sessions are stateless tokens with a flat 24-hour life and no refresh table. Revocation works
      by moving a timestamp on the user record: any token issued before it stops being accepted
      everywhere at once, which is what a password change needs to mean.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is exactly one way to cross a tenant boundary. A platform operator can act inside a
      named organisation by sending a header, checked against that organisation being active. It is
      the only impersonation path in the system, and it is deliberately the only one.
    </p>
    <p>
      Identity and health fields are encrypted with AES-256-GCM in the application before they reach
      a column, and in production the process refuses to start without a valid key rather than
      falling back to storing them in the clear. Uploads are validated by their leading signature
      bytes instead of the type the client claims, then re-encoded through an image pipeline that
      discards the original file entirely.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every unauthenticated endpoint carries its own rate limit, tuned per route rather than
      inherited from a global default — the public surface is the largest one here, so it gets the
      most specific treatment. The assistant runs a prompt-injection detector that marks a
      suspicious message and raises an alert before the message reaches a model. Nearly every state
      change writes an audit row: actor, action, entity, and a before-and-after snapshot.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="operations">
    <p>
      Every push to the main branch deploys. The pipeline connects to the host, resets the working
      tree to the exact commit that triggered it — verified by comparing the hash rather than
      trusting that a pull landed where it should — rebuilds the image without cache, and restarts
      the stack behind a health check.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then it does something slightly unusual: it searches the running container for a marker string
      that should only exist in freshly built assets. A deploy that reports success while quietly
      serving last week's files is worse than one that fails outright, because nobody goes looking
      for it. That check exists because it happened.
    </p>
    <p>
      The application container runs with a read-only root filesystem, as a non-root user, with
      privilege escalation disabled, and bound to localhost. The database is bound to localhost too
      and exposed to nothing else on the host.
    </p>
    <p>
      Retention runs as scheduled work inside the same process: photographs deleted after their
      window while the record they evidence is kept forever, guest contact details anonymised on a
      longer clock, idle sessions closed. On boot the process looks for any organisation left
      mid-reset by a crash and raises an alert, because the safe failure mode for a destructive
      operation is to stay locked and visible rather than to half-finish quietly.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="outcomes">
    <p>
      There are no user counts, tenant counts, or uptime figures on this page. They are not
      published anywhere I can point to, so stating them would mean estimating them, and an estimate
      dressed as a measurement is the thing this site is arguing against. What is below is what the
      system is configured to do, as of the last audit of its code.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="owner-input" set:html={ownerInputMarkers} />
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .lifecycle {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-8);
  }
</style>
